\n    41→\n    42→**Data flow:**\n    43→1. Extraction captures 250-char contexts (config.py:71)\n    44→2. BUT pairwise decision formats with max_chars=160/200 (consensus.py:991)\n    45→3. Death scene gets truncated\n    46→4. LLM receives incomplete context\n    47→5. LLM merges characters despite prompt rules\n    48→\n    49→### Result\n    50→**FAILED** - Smoke test showed 2 separate characters, but full analysis still merged them:\n    51→- Smoke test (12m 3s): 2 characters detected separately\n    52→- Full analysis (7m 45s): 1 character with \"the mummer\" as alias of \"Prince Prospero\"\n    53→\n    54→### Key Evidence\n    55→Full analysis output:\n    56→```\n    57→👥 Characters: 1\n    58→   • the Prince Prospero (aka Prospero, the mummer) - 7 mentions\n    59→```\n    60→\n    61→The merge still occurs despite the context window increase. The smoke test success may have been due to different model behavior or randomness in the LLM responses.\n    62→\n    63→## Current Issues (Priority Order)\n    64→\n    65→### CRITICAL\n    66→1. **False character merge: \"the mummer\" merged with Prince Prospero**\n    67→   - Problem: \"the mummer\" is listed as an alias of Prince Prospero, but it refers to the Red Death personification - THE MAIN ANTAGONIST\n    68→   - Evidence from text:\n    69→     - \"seizing the mummer, whose tall figure stood erect and motionless within the shadow of the ebony clock\"\n    70→     - \"fell prostrate in death the Prince Prospero\" (Prospero DIES after confronting the mummer)\n    71→     - The mummer is \"dressed as the Red Death itself, draped in grave-cloths\"\n    72→     - When unmasked, the figure is \"untenanted by any tangible form\" (not a person at all)\n    73→   - Impact: The main antagonist is merged with the protagonist (-2 point character score minimum)\n    74→   - **FOURTEEN attempts have now failed to fix this issue**\n    75→   - **Status:** Neither cross-group resolution fixes NOR pairwise prompt rules have worked\n    76→\n    77→2. **Missing character: The Red Death / Masked Figure**\n    78→   - Problem: The antagonist of the story should be its own character entry\n    79→   - Aliases that should be grouped together:\n    80→     - \"the figure\" / \"the masked figure\"\n    81→     - \"the stranger\"\n    82→     - \"the intruder\"\n    83→     - \"the mummer\"\n    84→     - \"the Red Death\" (personification)\n    85→   - Evidence: Chapter summary CORRECTLY identifies two characters but character extraction merges them\n    86→   - Impact: Major character missing from analysis\n    87→\n    88→### HIGH\n    89→3. **Missing alias: \"the duke\" for Prince Prospero**\n    90→   - Problem: Text uses \"the duke\" to refer to Prospero: \"The tastes of the duke were peculiar\"\n    91→   - Location: Alias detection\n    92→\n    93→### MEDIUM\n    94→4. **Canonical name format includes leading article**\n    95→   - Problem: Character name is \"the Prince Prospero\" instead of \"Prince Prospero\"\n    96→   - Location: Character name normalization\n    97→\n    98→5. **Too many common words in pronunciation guide (30%+ false positives)**\n    99→   - Problem: Common words like \"dauntless\", \"chiming\", \"magnificence\", \"casements\", \"buffoons\" are flagged\n   100→   - Count: ~20-25 of 73 entries are false positives\n   101→   - Location: Pronunciation flagging threshold or common word filter\n   102→\n   103→6. **Foreign word false positive: \"decorum\"**\n   104→   - Problem: \"decorum\" flagged as foreign word - it's standard English (Latin-derived but fully assimilated)\n   105→   - Location: Foreign word detection\n   106→\n   107→## Root Cause Analysis: Summary After 14 Attempts\n   108→\n   109→### The Core Problem\n   110→The LLM decides \"the mummer\" = \"Prince Prospero\" despite all prompt-based fixes. 14 different approaches have been tried at various pipeline stages (cross-group resolution, pairwise prompt rules, context windows, death detection functions), and NONE have worked.\n   111→\n   112→### Where the Merge Is Likely Happening\n   113→Based on 14 failed attempts, the merge is happening at a level that prompt engineering cannot easily fix:\n   114→1. **Initial name extraction** - The proposers may be tagging \"the mummer\" contexts with \"Prospero\" from the start\n   115→2. **Heuristic-level matching** - There may be heuristic code that considers \"the mummer\" a descriptive epithet for any character in the same scene\n   116→3. **LLM overconfidence** - The LLM may be too confident in merging characters at a masquerade ball\n   117→\n   118→### What Has Been Tried (All Failed)\n   119→| Attempt | Fix Applied | Result |\n   120→|---------|-------------|--------|\n   121→| 1 | Cross-group epithet resolution | No effect |\n   122→| 2 | Proper name with article classification | Caused regression |\n   123→| 3 | CRITICAL RULE #5 about confrontation in prompt | No effect |\n   124→| 4 | `_entities_in_confrontation()` function | No effect |\n   125→| 5 | Solo pattern matching for confrontation | No effect |\n   126→| 6 | (Evaluation only) | No effect |\n   127→| 7 | Context window 150/120 -> 400 chars | No effect |\n   128→| 8 | Hard-coded death relationship detection | No effect |\n   129→| 9 | Context windows 600-800, death rule #6 | No effect |\n   130→| 10 | Death detection check ALL contexts | No effect |\n   131→| 11 | mention_context_chars 100 -> 200 | +0.15 score, merge still occurs |\n   132→| 12 | mention_context_chars 200 -> 250 | Works on small model, FAILS on production |\n   133→| 13 | Enhanced death relationship detection | Never called - merge happens earlier |\n   134→| 14 | Death/confrontation rules in pairwise prompt | No effect |\n   135→\n   136→## Fix History\n   137→\n   138→### Attempts 1-13\n   139→See previous EVALUATION_STATE.md entries and git history.\n   140→\n   141→### Attempt 14\n   142→- **Change:** Added death/confrontation rules to PAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM and PAIRWISE_ALIAS